00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:44.611
Get your picture taken.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:48.548
See if you can trick it on what
emotions you're feeling and then

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.785
query it, the game where you
actually can compete with

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:55.855
somebody else and then to see
what you know about reddit or

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.258
Wikipedia or a whole bunch
of huge data sources with

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.626
BigQuery.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:02.695
I hope you also got time to look
at the various partners that are

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:07.700
out there showing off solutions
or add-ons or other software

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.303
that works with Google Cloud.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:14.107
You know and it's really
valuable to me and rewarding to

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.142
see so many of you
talking with each other,

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.144
to see customers
giving sessions,

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:21.114
to see partners giving
sessions and sharing your own

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.116
experiences.

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:26.986
There's also one thing -- I
hope you'll do me a favor.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.122
You know, those of us who are
lucky enough to be on stage at

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:32.659
events like this are just the
most visible part of many, many,

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.027
many people's work.

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.396
Whether it's the people who
build the demos that you saw;

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.598
people who build the
products, the sessions;

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.701
the staging and the event people
who transformed an empty shell

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.870
of a building into
this amazing venue;

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.040
our wonderful creative partners
at Instrument who helped come up

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.375
with this amazing lighting
design that you have.

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.344
Would you join me in thanking
them before we dive into all

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.412
that?

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:57.550
[ Applause ]

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.185
Thank you, they
really are remarkable.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:01.521
Today is going to be a
little bit different.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.624
Today we're going to give you
deep dives into three critical

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:05.792
technology areas.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.495
You're going to hear from the
engineering leaders at Google

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.763
who are closest to these
technologies who have been

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:14.200
working to really build out what
we think of as Google and Google

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.201
Cloud.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.202
There's no marketing today.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.638
You're hearing directly
from engineers.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.241
We picked these topics because
we think they're the ones that

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:25.612
are most critical in the
evolution of Cloud over the next

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:28.481
-- not just year, be
the next several years.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.484
And frankly, because we're nerds
at heart and we think the stuff

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.186
is pretty cool.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.655
Our first speaker is Joe Kava.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.558
Joe runs all data center
operations at Google.

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.994
And, you know, they're actually
a kind of legendary team within

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:45.932
Google because we depend upon
what they do to an incredible

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:49.335
degree and it's so easy to
take for granted the amazing

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.437
innovations.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.374
So to give us a look inside
at Google data centers,

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:56.809
how we build them,
how we operate them,

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.811
and the kind of
innovation we do,

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.813
would you please join me
in welcoming to the stage,

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.814
Joe Kava, Joe?

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:06.819
&gt;&gt; Thank you, very much.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.029
Thanks, Greg.

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:17.564
Good morning, everyone.

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:19.232
My name is Joe Kava.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.669
And I'm responsible for the
teams that design, build,

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.905
and operate Google's
data centers worldwide.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.875
So today I would like to share
with you a little bit about our

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.477
culture and our guiding
principles, really,

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.614
what is the secret sauce that
makes Google data centers so

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:37.083
unique?

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:42.021
So, first, imagine your data
center whether it's a private

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:45.625
enterprise data center or
a multi-tenant co-location

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.627
facility.

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:51.831
But now you don't have to
imagine anymore because with

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:56.202
Google Cloud Platform, your data
center is Google's data centers.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:03.443
GCP gives you access to
high-performance computing

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:07.480
available around the world in
our highly secure and ultra

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.482
reliable data centers.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:13.786
So let me delve a little bit
into what makes Google data

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.955
centers so special,
why we're unique.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:20.693
First, the core principles of
our data center program are

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.930
availability, security,
and performance.

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:29.636
You know, at the core of our
program is our commitment to

00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:31.404
availability.

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:35.341
I've been doing this a long time
and I have evaluated literally

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:39.212
dozens if not hundreds of data
centers all over the world both

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.913
public and private.

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.282
And I can tell you,
without a doubt,

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:47.020
they are not all
created equally.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.458
First, when we say 7 by
24, we absolutely mean it.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:57.063
Our designs are fault tolerant
and hardened against failure,

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:01.034
our critical infrastructure
is concurrently maintainable.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:04.504
This means that when something
does break or when maintenance

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:08.174
is required, we can do that
without taking down any part of

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:10.009
our critical infrastructure.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.912
This makes it invisible to you.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:16.983
Next, we design our data centers
with an eye towards the future.

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:20.420
So we're always building in
hooks into our designs that

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.290
allow us to upgrade our systems
and our network so that we can

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:29.762
always keep ahead or -- and
keep current with the latest

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:32.165
platforms developments.

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:36.536
And lastly, we staff our data
centers 24 by 7 with Googlers,

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:40.406
our own team of Googlers that
have been intimately involved

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:44.444
with design, to construction,
commissioning of the systems,

00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:46.446
and operations of
the data centers.

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.584
So, second, security.

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.754
This is truly of utmost
importance in the Google data

00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:56.756
center program.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:05.465
Google's focus on security among
is our primary design criteria.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:10.369
Our physical security model
features layered security that

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:15.441
includes things like custom
design access cards, alarms,

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:19.312
vehicle access barriers, metal
detectors, perimeter fencing,

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:23.649
biometrics, and even laser
intrusion detection guarding the

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.952
data center floor.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:29.889
Our data centers are monitored
24 by 7 by high resolution

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.859
cameras internal and external.

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:36.729
They can detect and
track any intruders.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.966
And our data centers are
constantly patrolled by our

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:43.236
security teams that have
undergone rigorous background

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:46.506
investigations and training.

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.841
And as you get closer to
the data center floor,

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:52.912
and to our core network,
the security increases.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:56.315
Access to the data center floor
is only available through a

00:06:56.315 --> 00:07:01.354
secure corridor that implements
multifactor authentication so

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:05.458
you need an authorized badge and
biometric clearance to get on to

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.360
the data center floor.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.164
And only approved employees that
have a business need to be there

00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:14.367
ever get access to the data
center floor or our core

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:15.735
network.

00:07:15.735 --> 00:07:19.972
In fact, less than 1% of all
Googlers ever set foot on the

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.975
data center at Google.

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:27.046
We employ a very strict
end-to-end chain of custody for

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:31.551
our storage systems, from the
first time a hard drive goes

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:36.088
into a machine until it is
verified as clean, erased,

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:41.027
or destroyed, we know every
movement of that hard drive.

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:46.699
We know who, the person; when,
the time stamps; we know what,

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:49.836
the serial number and the
state of the media; and why,

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:52.872
its next expected action.

00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:55.975
And information security and
physical security go hand in

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:58.978
hand in our data centers.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:02.582
You know, data is most
vulnerable when it's travelling

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:06.652
across the Internet
or within networks.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:09.722
And for this reason, securing
data in transit is a high

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:12.325
priority to Google.

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:15.661
Data travelling between a
customer's device and a Google

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:18.731
data center is encrypted
using httpstls.

00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:24.237
And as a matter of fact, Google
was the first major cloud

00:08:24.237 --> 00:08:26.973
provider to make this
default by standard.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.413
Our last guiding
principle is performance.

00:08:34.413 --> 00:08:36.983
You know, we've been leaders
in developing ultra high

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:39.719
performance compute systems
since the earliest days at

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:41.721
Google.

00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:46.325
Our high-performance computing
is the result of optimizing

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:48.628
throughout the entire stack.

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:52.131
Nearly all of our infrastructure
is custom-designed and

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:55.368
purpose-built for
our computing needs.

00:08:55.368 --> 00:08:59.305
From our own dedicated
high-voltage subsystems on site

00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:03.509
to the electrical distribution
systems and our cooling systems,

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:06.212
they're all working in
conjunction to provide the

00:09:06.212 --> 00:09:09.615
highest level of performance
and the lowest total cost of

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.617
ownership.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:15.755
When I first joined Google,
I was kind of amazed at the

00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:19.558
extraordinary lengths that the
company goes to to develop our

00:09:19.558 --> 00:09:21.994
own computing systems.

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:25.431
And, of course, over time I
learned why, but at the time,

00:09:25.431 --> 00:09:27.366
it wasn't so intuitive to me.

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:29.368
Why do we build our own servers?

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:31.370
Why not just buy?

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.908
Well, we go through all of this
extraordinary effort to create

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:40.913
our own because frankly, what we
needed at our scale didn't exist

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:42.515
when we started.

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:46.285
To achieve the performance
efficiency and price targets

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:49.722
that were necessary, we had
to build our own servers,

00:09:49.722 --> 00:09:53.326
we had to invent hardware, we
had to develop the software,

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:58.998
we had to create a culture
for scale and reliability.

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:02.468
Google servers don't include any
unnecessary components such as

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:07.073
video cards, chip sets, or
peripheral devices which can

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:10.209
introduce vulnerabilities.

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:13.879
Our production servers run a
custom-designed operating system

00:10:13.879 --> 00:10:17.883
based on a stripped down and
hardened version of Linux.

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:22.054
And Google's IP data network
consists of our own fiber,

00:10:22.054 --> 00:10:25.825
public fiber, and
undersea cables.

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:28.160
This allows us to
deliver highly available,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.564
low latency services
across the globe.

00:10:31.564 --> 00:10:35.234
But it also means that we can
respond faster to demand and

00:10:35.234 --> 00:10:37.236
provide the capacity you need.

00:10:40.873 --> 00:10:44.744
From my earliest days at Google,
our data center program has been

00:10:44.744 --> 00:10:47.580
as much committed to our
culture as it has been to our

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.515
principles.

00:10:49.515 --> 00:10:53.452
The key tenants of our
culture are ownership.

00:10:53.452 --> 00:10:56.055
Sustainability, and innovation.

00:10:59.091 --> 00:11:01.794
We have Googlers that
operate our data centers.

00:11:01.794 --> 00:11:04.797
You see, the norm in the
industry is for the design build

00:11:04.797 --> 00:11:09.502
contractor to hand over a set
of drawings and a set of owners

00:11:09.502 --> 00:11:12.905
manuals and the keys to the
front door and wish the data

00:11:12.905 --> 00:11:16.108
center operator, good luck.

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:20.646
Well, and all too often,
frankly, those operations teams,

00:11:20.646 --> 00:11:22.915
they aren't even
employed by the owner.

00:11:22.915 --> 00:11:26.052
They're outsourced to
the lowest cost bidder.

00:11:26.052 --> 00:11:29.922
And if there's on certainty in
data center operations it's that

00:11:29.922 --> 00:11:32.992
problems and faults are going
to happen in the middle of the

00:11:32.992 --> 00:11:36.929
night, typically on Sundays,
when no one else is available to

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:38.798
help out.

00:11:38.798 --> 00:11:41.700
So we take a different approach.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.904
Firstly, we hire the best and
the brightest engineers in

00:11:44.904 --> 00:11:49.141
operations professionals that
come from diverse backgrounds

00:11:49.141 --> 00:11:51.310
but they all have
one common trait,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.413
they are systems thinkers.

00:11:54.413 --> 00:11:56.749
Many of our team members
come from mission critical

00:11:56.749 --> 00:12:00.686
environments like the navy
nuclear submarine program where

00:12:00.686 --> 00:12:02.388
mistakes can be catastrophic.

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:06.892
They understand how systems
interact and how they work

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:08.627
together.

00:12:08.627 --> 00:12:12.698
Further, we've built regional
teams at all of our data center

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:16.402
Campuses that are comprised of
the engineers responsible for

00:12:16.402 --> 00:12:20.372
design and construction, working
side-by-side with the operations

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:21.907
teams.

00:12:21.907 --> 00:12:24.677
The experience and
qualifications of our staff is

00:12:24.677 --> 00:12:26.145
unparalleled.

00:12:26.145 --> 00:12:29.281
The majority of our staff
hold advanced degrees,

00:12:29.281 --> 00:12:32.818
they have professional licenses,
they're published in technical

00:12:32.818 --> 00:12:36.922
papers and in books, they
hold patents relative to our

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:40.493
industry, and together,
these integrated teams are

00:12:40.493 --> 00:12:43.262
responsible for
building the capacity,

00:12:43.262 --> 00:12:47.166
commissioning the systems, and
operating these data centers,

00:12:47.166 --> 00:12:49.568
7 by 24.

00:12:49.568 --> 00:12:53.706
This gives us an unparalleled
level of ownership end-to-end of

00:12:53.706 --> 00:12:55.674
our infrastructure.

00:12:57.877 --> 00:13:00.479
But for these teams to
truly be successful,

00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:03.349
they need to have advanced
realtime visibility into the

00:13:03.349 --> 00:13:07.686
status and the functionality
of our infrastructure.

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:10.856
As you know, to say that Google
is obsessed with data might be a

00:13:10.856 --> 00:13:13.225
little bit of an understatement.

00:13:13.225 --> 00:13:16.295
So to aid our teams, we built
world class monitoring and

00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:19.865
control systems for all
of the functional areas,

00:13:19.865 --> 00:13:23.836
from the servers and storage
and networking systems to our

00:13:23.836 --> 00:13:27.840
electrical distribution systems
and the security systems.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.344
We're monitoring all aspects
of performance and operations,

00:13:32.344 --> 00:13:34.480
truly from chip to chiller.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.152
But equally important to
our culture of ownership and

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:44.356
transparency -- I'm sorry --
equally important to our culture

00:13:44.356 --> 00:13:47.493
of ownership is transparency
and continual learning.

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:51.864
So when issues do occur, we
learn from them and we can share

00:13:51.864 --> 00:13:54.733
the learning with all of
our teams around the world.

00:13:54.733 --> 00:13:58.137
You know, we studied the
root cause of failures in the

00:13:58.137 --> 00:14:00.139
industry based on
published data.

00:14:00.139 --> 00:14:04.443
And on the infrastructure side,
the industry norm is that human

00:14:04.443 --> 00:14:08.080
error still accounts for
the overwhelming majority of

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:10.216
incidents.

00:14:10.216 --> 00:14:14.720
However, because of our designs
and our highly qualified people,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.923
only a very small fractions of
our incidents were related to

00:14:17.923 --> 00:14:20.559
human error and none of
them caused downtime.

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.334
You know, for as long as I've
been building and operating data

00:14:28.334 --> 00:14:31.604
centers, I've been an
advocate for efficiency and

00:14:31.604 --> 00:14:33.706
sustainability.

00:14:33.706 --> 00:14:36.442
It takes a tremendous amount
of power to run at our scale.

00:14:36.442 --> 00:14:39.645
And we have to be good
stewards for the environment,

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:42.815
to minimize our impact,
and reduce our consumption.

00:14:46.919 --> 00:14:49.488
We've been committed to
sustainability since the

00:14:49.488 --> 00:14:51.924
earliest days of our
data center program.

00:14:51.924 --> 00:14:55.060
We've been carbon
neutral since 2007.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:58.230
We've received an A-grade from
the carbon disclosure project

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.366
for the last two years running.

00:15:00.366 --> 00:15:05.371
All of our sites are ISO-14001
and 50001 certified for

00:15:05.371 --> 00:15:09.141
environmental protection and
energy management and we're

00:15:09.141 --> 00:15:12.544
consistently at the top
of Greenpeace's cool IT

00:15:12.544 --> 00:15:14.513
leaderboard.

00:15:14.513 --> 00:15:18.284
You know, shamefully, though,
sustainability doesn't matter to

00:15:18.284 --> 00:15:20.653
a lot to folks in our industry.

00:15:20.653 --> 00:15:24.023
It isn't even a criteria in
Gartner's Magic Quadrant for

00:15:24.023 --> 00:15:26.759
data center cloud providers.

00:15:26.759 --> 00:15:28.360
Well, it does matter.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:29.795
It matters to me.

00:15:29.795 --> 00:15:31.297
It matters to many of you.

00:15:31.297 --> 00:15:33.966
And it should matter to them.

00:15:33.966 --> 00:15:36.435
[ Applause ]

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:41.173
So let's -- thank you.

00:15:41.173 --> 00:15:42.741
So let's work together.

00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:44.910
Let's send a message to Gartner.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.545
Have them get onboard.

00:15:46.545 --> 00:15:49.214
Let's make this important
and let's make it a criteria.

00:15:51.984 --> 00:15:55.854
And for us, energy efficiency is
truly at the heart of our data

00:15:55.854 --> 00:15:57.890
center sustainability program.

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:01.293
We believe that the best energy
saved is the energy not consumed

00:16:01.293 --> 00:16:02.828
in the first place.

00:16:02.828 --> 00:16:06.165
That's why we've pioneered
hyperefficient computing systems

00:16:06.165 --> 00:16:08.267
and data centers.

00:16:08.267 --> 00:16:12.338
And for that fact, our data
centers consume on average only

00:16:12.338 --> 00:16:17.543
half of the energy compared to a
typical enterprise data center.

00:16:17.543 --> 00:16:21.447
This has enabled us to be the
first cloud provider to offer

00:16:21.447 --> 00:16:23.949
pricing based on
Moore's Law Scale.

00:16:28.053 --> 00:16:31.256
But we are equally committed to
powering our infrastructure with

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:33.092
renewable energy.

00:16:33.092 --> 00:16:37.596
As a matter of fact, we are the
world's largest private investor

00:16:37.596 --> 00:16:39.365
in renewable energy.

00:16:39.365 --> 00:16:42.968
To date, we've invested more
than $2 billion in power

00:16:42.968 --> 00:16:44.603
purchase agreements.

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:48.407
These PPAs are very
important because, one,

00:16:48.407 --> 00:16:54.213
we're buying the entire output
of wind and solar farms for long

00:16:54.213 --> 00:16:57.116
periods of time,
typically 10 to 20 years.

00:16:57.116 --> 00:17:01.520
These winds farms are on the
same power grids as our data

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:02.588
centers.

00:17:02.588 --> 00:17:06.458
And this gives the project developer the financial

00:17:06.458 --> 00:17:09.261
commitment and certainty
they need to get the project

00:17:09.261 --> 00:17:12.564
developed and so we know
that our investment is adding

00:17:12.564 --> 00:17:16.135
renewable power to the grid that
otherwise would not have been

00:17:16.135 --> 00:17:18.137
made available.

00:17:20.239 --> 00:17:24.176
And lastly, at the heart of
our culture is innovation.

00:17:24.176 --> 00:17:26.645
No matter how good our
systems are performing,

00:17:26.645 --> 00:17:28.747
no matter how well our
teams are performing,

00:17:28.747 --> 00:17:32.284
we always believe
we can do better.

00:17:32.284 --> 00:17:35.487
We have a relentless
focus on innovation.

00:17:35.487 --> 00:17:37.489
In fact, since I've
been at Google,

00:17:37.489 --> 00:17:40.759
we have redesigned our
fundamental cooling technology

00:17:40.759 --> 00:17:43.095
on average every
12 to 18 months.

00:17:43.095 --> 00:17:46.932
And along the way, we've
pioneered innovations in

00:17:46.932 --> 00:17:50.469
water-based cooling systems such
as sea water cooling for data

00:17:50.469 --> 00:17:56.608
centers, industrial canal water,
recycled or reuse water cooling

00:17:56.608 --> 00:18:01.313
systems, rain water harvesting,
storm water capture and reuse,

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:04.316
and thermal energy
storage systems.

00:18:04.316 --> 00:18:07.052
And we've designed data centers
that don't use water cooling at

00:18:07.052 --> 00:18:08.053
all.

00:18:08.053 --> 00:18:11.156
Instead they use 100%
outside air cooled.

00:18:11.156 --> 00:18:15.427
So the point here is that there
is no one size fits all model at

00:18:15.427 --> 00:18:16.495
Google.

00:18:16.495 --> 00:18:20.165
Each data center is designed
for the highest performance,

00:18:20.165 --> 00:18:24.903
the highest efficiency for
that specific location.

00:18:24.903 --> 00:18:28.073
We don't rest on our laurels.

00:18:28.073 --> 00:18:31.510
We've also invested a lot in
robotic innovation for our data

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:32.644
centers.

00:18:32.644 --> 00:18:36.815
This is one example of a fully
automated disc erase environment

00:18:36.815 --> 00:18:40.118
which allows for faster, more
efficient throughput and better

00:18:40.118 --> 00:18:42.721
inventory management
of our hard drives.

00:18:45.257 --> 00:18:48.760
And lately, we've pioneered the
use of machine learning and deep

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:52.231
learning algorithms for
data center operations.

00:18:52.231 --> 00:18:54.633
As you can imagine, our data
centers are very large and

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:56.134
complex.

00:18:56.134 --> 00:18:59.271
They are electrical and
mechanical in control systems

00:18:59.271 --> 00:19:03.008
all working together to try
to get the best performance.

00:19:03.008 --> 00:19:05.277
But the sheer number of
interactions and operating

00:19:05.277 --> 00:19:09.248
parameters makes it really
impossible for us mere mortals

00:19:09.248 --> 00:19:13.285
to understand how to optimize
a data center in realtime.

00:19:13.285 --> 00:19:16.355
However, it really is pretty
trivial for computers to crunch

00:19:16.355 --> 00:19:20.626
through all of those scenarios
and find the optimal settings.

00:19:20.626 --> 00:19:22.594
So, over the past
couple of years,

00:19:22.594 --> 00:19:25.764
we've developed these algorithms
and we've trained them with

00:19:25.764 --> 00:19:29.268
billions of data points from all
of our data centers all over the

00:19:29.268 --> 00:19:31.136
world.

00:19:31.136 --> 00:19:35.040
Now we use this machine learning
to help our teams visualize the

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:39.077
data so the operations teams
can know how to set up the

00:19:39.077 --> 00:19:42.447
electrical and mechanical plants
for the optimal settings on that

00:19:42.447 --> 00:19:43.448
given day.

00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:47.452
And often these consider up to
19 independent variables that

00:19:47.452 --> 00:19:49.187
affect performance.

00:19:49.187 --> 00:19:52.791
This helps our team identify
any discontinuities or any

00:19:52.791 --> 00:19:56.528
inflection points in the curves
that might not be intuitive

00:19:56.528 --> 00:19:58.530
otherwise.

00:20:01.366 --> 00:20:04.970
Now, as you can imagine, with
the explosive growth in public

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:08.607
-- in public cloud computing,
we've had some fairly aggressive

00:20:08.607 --> 00:20:11.176
expansion going on ourselves.

00:20:11.176 --> 00:20:13.512
So let's take a look
at a short video,

00:20:13.512 --> 00:20:18.216
never before seen in public, of
our data center campus in Iowa.

00:20:18.216 --> 00:20:22.120
Roll the video, please?

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:55.787
[ Music ]

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:56.888
Okay.

00:20:56.888 --> 00:20:59.825
What you're looking at
right there --

00:20:59.825 --> 00:21:01.059
[ Applause ]

00:21:01.059 --> 00:21:02.160
Thank you, thank you very much.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.696
Yeah, come on.

00:21:04.696 --> 00:21:07.065
I mean, who doesn't love
data centers, right?

00:21:07.065 --> 00:21:11.870
What you're looking at right
here is the world's largest data

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:13.438
center campus.

00:21:13.438 --> 00:21:15.273
Look at the size of the trucks.

00:21:15.273 --> 00:21:17.075
Those are construction trucks.

00:21:17.075 --> 00:21:19.444
Look at them for scale.

00:21:19.444 --> 00:21:23.782
Each of these building pads is
over a third of a mile long with

00:21:23.782 --> 00:21:26.485
multistoried data centers.

00:21:26.485 --> 00:21:30.322
We know that long-term capacity
planning is very difficult.

00:21:30.322 --> 00:21:32.224
Everyone struggles with it.

00:21:32.224 --> 00:21:35.861
And we have been growing and
keep growing to meet your needs

00:21:35.861 --> 00:21:37.729
and keep ahead of your needs.

00:21:37.729 --> 00:21:40.465
So with capacity like this,
when you're ready to grow,

00:21:40.465 --> 00:21:42.467
we'll be ready for you.

00:21:46.371 --> 00:21:47.372
[ Applause ]

00:21:47.372 --> 00:21:50.642
Thank you.

00:21:50.642 --> 00:21:52.644
Yeah. Okay.

00:21:52.644 --> 00:21:55.614
So, today we have 13 operational
data center campuses on four

00:21:55.614 --> 00:21:57.983
continents and there are
two more currently under

00:21:57.983 --> 00:21:59.951
development.

00:21:59.951 --> 00:22:03.588
Our global network brings our
data centers closer together and

00:22:03.588 --> 00:22:07.859
closer to you, with more than
70 points of presence in 33

00:22:07.859 --> 00:22:08.894
countries.

00:22:08.894 --> 00:22:12.698
This ensures the same low
latency and responsiveness that

00:22:12.698 --> 00:22:16.635
you expect from Google's own
services is now available to you

00:22:16.635 --> 00:22:18.637
on GCP also.

00:22:20.338 --> 00:22:24.776
And cloud platform is available
at these locations including our

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:27.879
newly announced Japan
and Oregon regions,

00:22:27.879 --> 00:22:31.416
and there's ten more that
are coming throughout 2017.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:38.523
And lastly, I'd like to remind
you about our new 360 tour of

00:22:38.523 --> 00:22:40.826
our data center in Oregon.

00:22:40.826 --> 00:22:42.394
It's really cool.

00:22:42.394 --> 00:22:45.864
It's like a virtual walkthrough
of one of Google's data centers.

00:22:45.864 --> 00:22:48.567
It's available in the
playground area outside.

00:22:48.567 --> 00:22:52.771
And for those on livestream, you
can experience it for yourself

00:22:52.771 --> 00:22:55.240
at the following link.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.142
So, thank you all very much.

00:22:57.142 --> 00:22:59.745
It's been a pleasure
being with you here today.

00:22:59.745 --> 00:23:01.213
Thank you.

00:23:01.213 --> 00:23:08.320
[ Applause ]

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:09.955
&gt;&gt; You know, one of the fun
things about working at Google

00:23:09.955 --> 00:23:12.491
is working with people who are
passionate about what they do.

00:23:12.491 --> 00:23:14.793
And I think that just comes
across so clearly that Joe is

00:23:14.793 --> 00:23:18.864
passionate about his work.

00:23:18.864 --> 00:23:21.700
So, that's data centers.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.736
Our second speaker in our
second topic is about security.

00:23:24.736 --> 00:23:28.140
Niels Provos is a distinguished
engineer at Google focused on

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:29.775
security and privacy.

00:23:29.775 --> 00:23:33.111
Now for context, there are only
about 50 distinguished engineers

00:23:33.111 --> 00:23:35.347
inside all of Google.

00:23:35.347 --> 00:23:37.849
Now -- and according
to his Wikipedia page,

00:23:37.849 --> 00:23:40.652
in his spare time he
turns steel into swords.

00:23:43.722 --> 00:23:46.491
You know, security is different
than many other features.

00:23:46.491 --> 00:23:49.327
It's an opportunity for
us to work with you,

00:23:49.327 --> 00:23:54.833
to create the best possible
security environment for you,

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:56.134
your customers.

00:23:56.134 --> 00:23:59.404
And with GCP, we have a great
opportunity to put more and more

00:23:59.404 --> 00:24:02.974
of the tools that we use to
manage our security into your

00:24:02.974 --> 00:24:06.444
hands so that you can manage
your security as well.

00:24:06.444 --> 00:24:08.480
So with that, I'm really
delighted and honored to bring

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:10.949
up on stage, Niels Provos,
distinguished engineer from

00:24:10.949 --> 00:24:11.950
Google.

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:12.951
Niels?

00:24:12.951 --> 00:24:17.956
&gt;&gt; Thank you for the
introduction, Greg.

00:24:25.363 --> 00:24:26.932
Good morning, everybody.

00:24:26.932 --> 00:24:29.835
As Greg said, I'm a
distinguished engineer at

00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:30.836
Google.

00:24:30.836 --> 00:24:33.505
I am responsible for the
security and privacy engineering

00:24:33.505 --> 00:24:34.506
teams.

00:24:34.506 --> 00:24:38.009
Today I would like to pull back
the covers a little bit and

00:24:38.009 --> 00:24:41.913
share with you a glimpse on
how we pervasively secure you,

00:24:41.913 --> 00:24:46.852
your data, your customers' data,
and your applications when you

00:24:46.852 --> 00:24:50.021
run on top of Google's
Cloud Platform.

00:24:50.021 --> 00:24:53.191
However, let me set the
stage a little bit first.

00:24:53.191 --> 00:24:57.028
At the recent Enigma Conference
in San Francisco, Rob Joyce,

00:24:57.028 --> 00:25:01.566
the head of NSA's tailored
access operations made the

00:25:01.566 --> 00:25:06.171
following statements on how
advanced adversaries managed to

00:25:06.171 --> 00:25:08.340
break into your
systems and networks.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.110
Now I understand that most of
you would like to spend your

00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:16.448
time and focus on building a
great product and great new

00:25:16.448 --> 00:25:18.850
features for your customers.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:22.821
However, your adversaries, they
focus their time and energy on

00:25:22.821 --> 00:25:26.992
finding weak spots in your
systems that they can exploit.

00:25:26.992 --> 00:25:31.196
Fortunately, at Google I lead
a team of experts who builds

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:34.332
systems and processes
specifically designed to thwart

00:25:34.332 --> 00:25:35.667
adversaries.

00:25:35.667 --> 00:25:38.737
We do this by anticipating
them, preventing them,

00:25:38.737 --> 00:25:42.507
protecting them, disrupting
them, and containing them.

00:25:42.507 --> 00:25:45.377
And we will work with you to
make sure that you can meet your

00:25:45.377 --> 00:25:48.413
requirements and that you
can leverage our security

00:25:48.413 --> 00:25:50.715
capabilities on top
of the platform.

00:25:50.715 --> 00:25:53.718
So now I would like to give you
a little more insight on how we

00:25:53.718 --> 00:25:57.155
think about security at Google
and how we have created a secure

00:25:57.155 --> 00:25:59.124
infrastructure for ourself.

00:25:59.124 --> 00:26:03.161
Google security and privacy team
consists of over 500 people who

00:26:03.161 --> 00:26:06.564
continuously work to protect
our infrastructure and you when

00:26:06.564 --> 00:26:08.633
you're running on top of GCP.

00:26:08.633 --> 00:26:12.737
Due to our scale, we end up
seeing all kinds of bad behavior

00:26:12.737 --> 00:26:16.474
across the internet, phishing
e-mails, malware websites,

00:26:16.474 --> 00:26:17.676
network attacks.

00:26:17.676 --> 00:26:20.845
We also see all kinds of
adversaries ranging from all

00:26:20.845 --> 00:26:24.349
levels of sophistication,
not just script kiddies and

00:26:24.349 --> 00:26:27.886
cybercriminals, but also
nation-state actors.

00:26:27.886 --> 00:26:32.657
Now at Google, we are not
pursuing a hard shell pyramidal

00:26:32.657 --> 00:26:33.224
model.

00:26:33.224 --> 00:26:36.728
Instead, we are really focused
on pervasive defense and depth.

00:26:36.728 --> 00:26:40.131
This is similar to the immune
system analogy that Urs

00:26:40.131 --> 00:26:41.166
mentioned yesterday.

00:26:41.166 --> 00:26:45.503
There are two key principles
running pervasively to our

00:26:45.503 --> 00:26:47.105
approach to security.

00:26:47.105 --> 00:26:49.174
And that is trust
and transparency.

00:26:49.174 --> 00:26:53.411
That means that we design for
trust throughout our stack and

00:26:53.411 --> 00:26:57.515
we build in the transparency
so that you can verify for

00:26:57.515 --> 00:26:58.083
yourself.

00:26:58.083 --> 00:27:01.052
And, in fact, this talk is one
of our steps in giving you more

00:27:01.052 --> 00:27:03.588
transparency.

00:27:03.588 --> 00:27:06.591
Now, I'll be talking about
Google's infrastructure in the

00:27:06.591 --> 00:27:08.426
first part of the
talk, you know,

00:27:08.426 --> 00:27:09.728
how we think about
security there.

00:27:09.728 --> 00:27:11.663
And then in the second
part of the talk,

00:27:11.663 --> 00:27:14.032
I will explain how that applies
to you when you run on top of

00:27:14.032 --> 00:27:15.700
GCP.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:19.170
In order to facilitate
this discussion,

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:22.874
we have split our stack
into eight different layers.

00:27:22.874 --> 00:27:25.176
And I'll be walking from
the bottom up, you know,

00:27:25.176 --> 00:27:28.246
starting with the hardware to
the boot process all the way up

00:27:28.246 --> 00:27:30.281
to the deployment,
operations, and usage.

00:27:32.317 --> 00:27:36.788
At the hardware layer, we are
fortunate enough that we operate

00:27:36.788 --> 00:27:41.059
at such a skill that we can
build our own proprietary secure

00:27:41.059 --> 00:27:42.394
systems.

00:27:42.394 --> 00:27:44.729
That means that we have
purposeful hardware for

00:27:44.729 --> 00:27:49.034
computing, storage, networking,
all the way through the full

00:27:49.034 --> 00:27:51.970
data centers as you just
heard Joe talk about.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:56.141
Our ability to design and deploy
custom hardware means that we

00:27:56.141 --> 00:28:00.111
can maintain provenance and
establish trust at the hardware

00:28:00.111 --> 00:28:01.679
level.

00:28:01.679 --> 00:28:03.348
Now, on top of this
trusted hardware,

00:28:03.348 --> 00:28:07.585
it's important that we can
also boot in a trusted way.

00:28:07.585 --> 00:28:10.922
And to accomplish that, we
have employed code signing that

00:28:10.922 --> 00:28:15.460
allows us to verify the BIOS,
the boot loader and the kernel.

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:19.164
And that means that only the
intended software can run on

00:28:19.164 --> 00:28:21.466
Google's infrastructure.

00:28:21.466 --> 00:28:24.969
So now that we know that our
machines have booted in the

00:28:24.969 --> 00:28:29.174
known good state, it's also
important that all of our jobs

00:28:29.174 --> 00:28:33.611
communicate in an authenticated
and authorized fashion.

00:28:33.611 --> 00:28:36.214
To accomplish that, we have
built a cryptographic key

00:28:36.214 --> 00:28:40.151
distribution system called Laws
that allows us to establish

00:28:40.151 --> 00:28:43.588
cryptographic identities
for all jobs that run on our

00:28:43.588 --> 00:28:45.190
infrastructure.

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:49.160
We know and audit the binaries
that run so we can establish

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:53.431
trust not only at the operating
system but also for the Apps

00:28:53.431 --> 00:28:54.532
that are running on it.

00:28:54.532 --> 00:28:58.736
Now, we have heard a lot
about virtual machines.

00:28:58.736 --> 00:29:01.372
And one of the properties we
really need there is secure

00:29:01.372 --> 00:29:02.540
isolation.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:06.344
At Google, we use KVM as our
hypervisor technology and we are

00:29:06.344 --> 00:29:09.647
significantly invested
to harden the security.

00:29:09.647 --> 00:29:15.253
That means that our security
teams have found 15 of the last

00:29:15.253 --> 00:29:18.456
21 in publicly known
vulnerabilities in KVM.

00:29:18.456 --> 00:29:21.993
That means that we are the first
to fix, and the first to patch,

00:29:21.993 --> 00:29:25.196
but, of course, we also
contribute our findings back to

00:29:25.196 --> 00:29:29.601
the community so that everybody
can benefit from a more secure

00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:32.103
KVM technology.

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:35.573
We continue on working to
make KVM even more secure.

00:29:37.775 --> 00:29:41.346
To get a secure operating
system imagine on our machines,

00:29:41.346 --> 00:29:45.250
we have created a Google curated
image that we run throughout our

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:46.784
production infrastructure.

00:29:46.784 --> 00:29:50.622
This is very similar to the
recommendation from the NSA of

00:29:50.622 --> 00:29:52.824
having a secure baseline.

00:29:52.824 --> 00:29:55.693
We have also deployed a
service that alerts us of

00:29:55.693 --> 00:29:58.930
vulnerabilities and third party
source code to further protect

00:29:58.930 --> 00:30:01.299
our services.

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:06.004
Now at the storage layer, it's
really important that all access

00:30:06.004 --> 00:30:09.474
to data is individually
authorized and ACL'd.

00:30:09.474 --> 00:30:13.545
Before we write data to disk, we
chunk it and spread it so that

00:30:13.545 --> 00:30:17.115
not all data ends up
on the same server.

00:30:17.115 --> 00:30:21.319
We also encrypt data, and
metadata individually and to

00:30:21.319 --> 00:30:24.622
protect these encryption keys,
we have built a system called

00:30:24.622 --> 00:30:28.626
Keystore that wraps them
by encrypting them further.

00:30:28.626 --> 00:30:31.996
Our storage systems only install
this wrap key and never the

00:30:31.996 --> 00:30:35.667
original cryptographic key that
will be required to decrypt the

00:30:35.667 --> 00:30:40.004
data and that means that
all access to data requires

00:30:40.004 --> 00:30:43.575
interaction with Keystore which
gives us logging and auditing

00:30:43.575 --> 00:30:45.009
capabilities.

00:30:45.009 --> 00:30:48.980
Similar, for data in motion,
we encrypted when the troubles

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:51.516
between systems that we control.

00:30:51.516 --> 00:30:55.453
We accomplish fine grain control
over access with our internal

00:30:55.453 --> 00:30:59.357
IM systems and they also give us logging and auditing

00:30:59.357 --> 00:31:00.825
capabilities.

00:31:00.825 --> 00:31:03.561
So, now at this point, we are
running on trusted hardware.

00:31:03.561 --> 00:31:06.798
We know that the boot process
was trusted and we trust our

00:31:06.798 --> 00:31:09.801
operating system and we also
have provenance for hardware

00:31:09.801 --> 00:31:12.136
boot and wares.

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:14.939
We encrypt the data and we know
that all access is authenticated

00:31:14.939 --> 00:31:17.809
and authorized.

00:31:17.809 --> 00:31:22.013
Now, to secure our applications,
we're pursuing multiple layered

00:31:22.013 --> 00:31:23.681
approaches.

00:31:23.681 --> 00:31:26.618
One of them is that we have
built a binary on configuration

00:31:26.618 --> 00:31:28.219
verification system.

00:31:28.219 --> 00:31:30.421
That means that for
all applications,

00:31:30.421 --> 00:31:33.791
we know how they were built, we
know how they were configured,

00:31:33.791 --> 00:31:35.994
and we know how
they were deployed.

00:31:35.994 --> 00:31:38.229
And for each of these steps,
we know who was responsible for

00:31:38.229 --> 00:31:39.530
them.

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:43.301
To put additional security
into our development process,

00:31:43.301 --> 00:31:46.337
we require that all
code gets peer reviewed.

00:31:46.337 --> 00:31:49.073
That means there's a second pair
of eyes that looks at the code

00:31:49.073 --> 00:31:51.943
before it's being committed
so that we can find bugs and

00:31:51.943 --> 00:31:54.612
security problems very early on.

00:31:54.612 --> 00:31:57.615
To help our teams create
more secure systems,

00:31:57.615 --> 00:32:01.853
we also require security review
before anything can launch.

00:32:01.853 --> 00:32:06.357
That means that we can put
security into our products right

00:32:06.357 --> 00:32:07.959
at the design phase.

00:32:07.959 --> 00:32:09.994
However, you know, even
with the best practices,

00:32:09.994 --> 00:32:12.830
you still cannot prevent
all security problems.

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:16.301
And so we have created a
security scanning technology

00:32:16.301 --> 00:32:19.304
that allows us to find many
of the common mistakes,

00:32:19.304 --> 00:32:22.373
cross side scripting, mixed
content, mixed pending,

00:32:22.373 --> 00:32:26.744
more strange attacks
such as a Rosetta Flash.

00:32:26.744 --> 00:32:31.516
And we have also used this as an
example where we can contribute

00:32:31.516 --> 00:32:32.884
back to the community.

00:32:32.884 --> 00:32:36.321
We have made our firing
range test bed open source.

00:32:36.321 --> 00:32:38.956
That is if anybody else would
like to develop their own

00:32:38.956 --> 00:32:43.328
security scanning technology,
we already have a ready-made App

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:44.896
that they can test against.

00:32:44.896 --> 00:32:47.999
So at this point, our
applications are reviewed,

00:32:47.999 --> 00:32:49.000
they've been tested.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.002
We believe they don't
have vulnerabilities,

00:32:51.002 --> 00:32:54.105
data is encrypted, and
access is authorized.

00:32:54.105 --> 00:32:56.874
But now it's time to expose
these applications to the

00:32:56.874 --> 00:32:58.509
dangerous web.

00:32:58.509 --> 00:33:01.312
There are a multiple different
ways in which we secure our

00:33:01.312 --> 00:33:02.313
applications there.

00:33:02.313 --> 00:33:06.651
First of all, on the network,
we protect the traffic with TLS.

00:33:06.651 --> 00:33:08.653
To prevent many of
the middle attacks,

00:33:08.653 --> 00:33:12.056
we have put certificate pinning
into our Chrome browser so that

00:33:12.056 --> 00:33:15.460
we only trust the
right certificates.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:19.564
We have also deployed
unphishable user identity to our

00:33:19.564 --> 00:33:23.468
users with unphishable hardware
second factors and to protect

00:33:23.468 --> 00:33:26.871
against network attacks, we
have built in DDoS protection.

00:33:26.871 --> 00:33:29.674
Some of you might have heard
recently about Project Shield

00:33:29.674 --> 00:33:32.577
where we announced that we're
extending this DDoS protection

00:33:32.577 --> 00:33:35.613
to news and human rights
organizations as well as

00:33:35.613 --> 00:33:37.715
election monitoring sites.

00:33:37.715 --> 00:33:39.650
[ Applause ]

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:43.154
Thank you.

00:33:43.154 --> 00:33:46.290
At the operations level, we have
built technology that allows us

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:50.762
to patch and monitor, do
incident response, forensics,

00:33:50.762 --> 00:33:53.030
threat analysis and
threat intelligence.

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:55.733
And this is also another example
where we have contributed back

00:33:55.733 --> 00:33:58.302
to the open source by making
some of our forensic tools

00:33:58.302 --> 00:34:03.608
available such as GRR or
recall for memory analytics.

00:34:03.608 --> 00:34:08.079
Now, one of the sayings that we
have is the first user who is

00:34:08.079 --> 00:34:11.048
going to be hitting your
application is likely going to

00:34:11.048 --> 00:34:12.316
be an App user.

00:34:12.316 --> 00:34:15.753
So we give our developers
the tools to be able to do

00:34:15.753 --> 00:34:17.922
reputation checks on
your apps and binaries.

00:34:17.922 --> 00:34:21.125
We have automated
analysis of traffic,

00:34:21.125 --> 00:34:23.795
and to find botnets that
might be engaging in spam.

00:34:23.795 --> 00:34:27.999
And we also leverage our global
view on botnets to build threat

00:34:27.999 --> 00:34:31.502
intelligence on bad actors
and current attack factors.

00:34:31.502 --> 00:34:36.774
However, to reduce the overall
attack surface that we face and

00:34:36.774 --> 00:34:38.910
that our users face
on the internet,

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:43.948
and that all of you face, we
created Project Zero in 2014.

00:34:43.948 --> 00:34:48.820
It's a team dedicated to finding
zero data vulnerabilities in

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:51.489
critical internet software.

00:34:51.489 --> 00:34:54.492
You may have heard about the
Heartbleed Bug in open SSL.

00:34:54.492 --> 00:34:56.494
Urs mentioned that
briefly yesterday,

00:34:56.494 --> 00:35:00.465
or about rowhammer where just
reading memory can lead to

00:35:00.465 --> 00:35:02.633
remote code execution.

00:35:02.633 --> 00:35:05.536
And most recently we announced
a widespread security

00:35:05.536 --> 00:35:06.604
vulnerability in GDIPC.

00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:11.142
So that means that we have
a team that finds, you know,

00:35:11.142 --> 00:35:14.879
critical security problems
that are very widely spread.

00:35:14.879 --> 00:35:16.948
And, you know, we get
the benefit of, you know,

00:35:16.948 --> 00:35:19.717
knowing about these first so we
can fix our infrastructure but

00:35:19.717 --> 00:35:22.653
we can also work with the rest
of the community to make sure

00:35:22.653 --> 00:35:25.590
that we get the internet
infrastructure, you know,

00:35:25.590 --> 00:35:27.558
fixed in general.

00:35:27.558 --> 00:35:28.960
As I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:32.096
you're writing secure
software is difficult.

00:35:32.096 --> 00:35:36.234
And Google has been one of the
first companies to create a

00:35:36.234 --> 00:35:39.971
vulnerability rewards program
where we ask researchers across

00:35:39.971 --> 00:35:45.276
the world with helping find
security bugs in our products

00:35:45.276 --> 00:35:49.680
and just last year we paid out
over $2 million to hundreds of

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.783
researchers who helped us
build more secure products.

00:35:52.783 --> 00:35:55.820
So now let's switch over to GCP.

00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:59.757
I have just told you and I hope
you understand a little better

00:35:59.757 --> 00:36:02.093
about the security capabilities
that we have built for

00:36:02.093 --> 00:36:03.094
ourselves.

00:36:03.094 --> 00:36:05.229
You might wonder, that's
great, but, you know,

00:36:05.229 --> 00:36:09.066
how does that apply to us
and when we run on GCP?

00:36:09.066 --> 00:36:12.603
These security capabilities
that I mentioned to you is what

00:36:12.603 --> 00:36:17.174
enables our Google
developers to lift, you know,

00:36:17.174 --> 00:36:20.811
the V3 cloud experience,
the next cloud experience.

00:36:20.811 --> 00:36:23.147
It means that they can
focus on building, you know,

00:36:23.147 --> 00:36:26.551
amazing products and they do
not have to be security experts.

00:36:26.551 --> 00:36:30.354
Now we understand that many of
you are on a spectrum on how you

00:36:30.354 --> 00:36:32.790
develop and manage your
applications and that all of you

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:36.193
are going to be at different
points in your journey to this

00:36:36.193 --> 00:36:37.995
next version of Cloud.

00:36:37.995 --> 00:36:40.965
We understand that you may
have to manage and segment your

00:36:40.965 --> 00:36:45.169
network to create trust zones or
you need more explicit control

00:36:45.169 --> 00:36:47.738
over the firewall of
the network parameter.

00:36:47.738 --> 00:36:51.475
All of you need logging
and auditing capabilities.

00:36:51.475 --> 00:36:54.879
And we are really committed
to help you meet your goals

00:36:54.879 --> 00:36:57.949
wherever you are on your journey
to this next version of the

00:36:57.949 --> 00:36:58.950
cloud.

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:02.753
And to accomplish that, we are
increasingly making more of

00:37:02.753 --> 00:37:06.157
these capabilities available to
you and I'll be explaining to

00:37:06.157 --> 00:37:10.127
you a little bit how that
is going to look like.

00:37:10.127 --> 00:37:12.129
So I mentioned, you
know, previously,

00:37:12.129 --> 00:37:14.565
we had eight layers in
recognition of the needs that

00:37:14.565 --> 00:37:17.468
many of you need better
control over the network.

00:37:17.468 --> 00:37:21.005
We have introduced the
network layer here.

00:37:21.005 --> 00:37:23.507
So, I mentioned to you what it
is that we do at the hardware

00:37:23.507 --> 00:37:26.544
level, what it is that we
do at our boot process,

00:37:26.544 --> 00:37:28.713
and for the operating
system level.

00:37:28.713 --> 00:37:32.483
You're actually benefitting off
all of these mechanisms already

00:37:32.483 --> 00:37:36.087
because GCP is built
on top of this.

00:37:36.087 --> 00:37:38.623
Many of you have also
compliance requirements and our

00:37:38.623 --> 00:37:42.960
infrastructure is regularly
audited by external auditors for

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:49.667
SOC 213, ISO 2701, PCI DSS
3.0 and for Google Compute and

00:37:49.667 --> 00:37:52.536
Google Cloud Storage,
Cloud SQL and BigQuery.

00:37:52.536 --> 00:37:54.739
We're also covered
under the HIPPA-BAA.

00:37:54.739 --> 00:37:58.009
That means that you can build
on top of an infrastructure

00:37:58.009 --> 00:38:02.713
foundation that is regularly
audited and certified.

00:38:02.713 --> 00:38:06.717
For cloud, at the storage level,
all data is already encrypted by

00:38:06.717 --> 00:38:08.285
default.

00:38:08.285 --> 00:38:11.956
To give you even greater control
on how data is being stored at

00:38:11.956 --> 00:38:14.892
rest, we have made customer
supplied encryption keys made

00:38:14.892 --> 00:38:17.461
available to you and we
also give you audit logging

00:38:17.461 --> 00:38:19.330
capabilities.

00:38:19.330 --> 00:38:22.800
In the future, we plan on giving
you even more control with a key

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:26.303
management system that allows
you to manage your own keys,

00:38:26.303 --> 00:38:30.074
you know, generate them, rotate
them, suspend, resume them,

00:38:30.074 --> 00:38:34.445
delete them, or manage the
import and export of secrets.

00:38:34.445 --> 00:38:37.348
Yesterday, you've heard about our IM system that we have not

00:38:37.348 --> 00:38:42.286
made available to you which gives you explicit role-based

00:38:42.286 --> 00:38:43.688
access control.

00:38:43.688 --> 00:38:46.724
And we will give you additional
capabilities later on in the

00:38:46.724 --> 00:38:48.726
year.

00:38:48.726 --> 00:38:53.798
Now at the network layer, many
of you require explicit control.

00:38:53.798 --> 00:38:56.701
In fact, today, there are
already many ways in which you

00:38:56.701 --> 00:38:59.970
can customize your network such
as with isolated IP groups,

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:03.841
private IP addresses,
BPNs, or inbound firewalls.

00:39:03.841 --> 00:39:07.945
We're working on extending
and expanding these features,

00:39:07.945 --> 00:39:10.548
including outbound firewalls.

00:39:12.683 --> 00:39:17.021
When you run on
Google App Engine,

00:39:17.021 --> 00:39:20.691
you already can benefit from
the security scanning technology

00:39:20.691 --> 00:39:24.295
that I mentioned to you earlier
with the Cloud security scanner.

00:39:24.295 --> 00:39:26.731
We're working on
creating more scanners,

00:39:26.731 --> 00:39:30.701
and on increasing the scanning
capabilities so you can also use

00:39:30.701 --> 00:39:32.803
it on GCE and GKE.

00:39:32.803 --> 00:39:36.507
We also plan to give you the
ability to sign your binaries so

00:39:36.507 --> 00:39:40.911
that you can verify and enforce
what gets to run as part of your

00:39:40.911 --> 00:39:42.413
infrastructure.

00:39:42.413 --> 00:39:45.049
We're also thinking of giving
you source code security

00:39:45.049 --> 00:39:48.586
capabilities; however, that
requires that you first store

00:39:48.586 --> 00:39:51.455
your source code with us, so
please check out the Google

00:39:51.455 --> 00:39:53.758
Cloud source repositories.

00:39:55.092 --> 00:39:59.663
For protecting your
developers and users,

00:39:59.663 --> 00:40:02.600
we already give you the
options of using security keys,

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:05.169
which gives you this, you know,
unphishable hardware second

00:40:05.169 --> 00:40:06.303
factor.

00:40:06.303 --> 00:40:09.273
Many of you at the moment may
be relying on this sort of,

00:40:09.273 --> 00:40:11.942
you know, hard network parameter
that I've been talking about

00:40:11.942 --> 00:40:13.043
earlier.

00:40:13.043 --> 00:40:16.447
We also plan on giving you
detailed and sophisticated

00:40:16.447 --> 00:40:18.883
identity and
authorization tools,

00:40:18.883 --> 00:40:21.786
that means that you can write
access policy not just based on

00:40:21.786 --> 00:40:24.622
the user, but also on
the machine and location.

00:40:24.622 --> 00:40:27.825
You may have heard about the
Beyond Corp model yesterday.

00:40:27.825 --> 00:40:29.493
That sort of goes
in that direction.

00:40:29.493 --> 00:40:33.264
When you leverage Google
scale by using, let's say,

00:40:33.264 --> 00:40:37.968
the https load balancer, you
also benefit from built-in DDoS

00:40:37.968 --> 00:40:39.270
protection.

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:43.908
To protect your SSL
and TLS certificates,

00:40:43.908 --> 00:40:46.877
we have been working for years
on something that is called

00:40:46.877 --> 00:40:51.048
certificate transparency, that
essentially creates publicly

00:40:51.048 --> 00:40:54.518
verifiable logs that give you
provenance and trust for the

00:40:54.518 --> 00:40:56.654
certificates for
your own domains.

00:41:01.458 --> 00:41:04.562
For operations, you already
benefit essentially from all the

00:41:04.562 --> 00:41:07.798
practices that we have for
the underlying infrastructure,

00:41:07.798 --> 00:41:09.600
where we, you know,
regularly patch and upgrade.

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.736
However for your own projects,
we are looking into giving you

00:41:12.736 --> 00:41:15.840
an integrated view that gives
you an idea of the patch level

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:18.609
of vulnerabilities for
your own deployments,

00:41:18.609 --> 00:41:21.345
and we're also thinking of ways
of adding some of our threat

00:41:21.345 --> 00:41:23.347
intelligence into that view.

00:41:25.482 --> 00:41:28.919
You already can benefit from
audit logging and stack driver

00:41:28.919 --> 00:41:32.256
to give you a better idea and
detailed records of what's going

00:41:32.256 --> 00:41:34.892
on with your infrastructure
and your data.

00:41:34.892 --> 00:41:38.162
In the future we plan on giving
you strong authors of how second

00:41:38.162 --> 00:41:40.598
factoring for logging
into your projects,

00:41:40.598 --> 00:41:44.635
but we're also investigating
additional app use signals that

00:41:44.635 --> 00:41:47.071
we might be able to make
available to you when you

00:41:47.071 --> 00:41:50.808
develop your applications, such
as URL and binary reputation.

00:41:50.808 --> 00:41:57.047
So these are just some of the
security capabilities that we

00:41:57.047 --> 00:42:02.253
will increasingly make available
to you to access and control.

00:42:02.253 --> 00:42:06.323
You already have the option for
us to manage the security and

00:42:06.323 --> 00:42:10.394
the platform, if you use, for
example, Google App Engine,

00:42:10.394 --> 00:42:14.231
but we also planned on giving
you the ability to leverage an

00:42:14.231 --> 00:42:18.235
increasing array of security
features that you can manage to

00:42:18.235 --> 00:42:19.904
your own requirements.

00:42:19.904 --> 00:42:23.073
And to be clear, our goal here
is to provide you the control

00:42:23.073 --> 00:42:27.144
you need on the trusted
and transparent Cloud,

00:42:27.144 --> 00:42:30.414
so that you do not have to
become security experts,

00:42:30.414 --> 00:42:34.385
but can instead focus on
innovation and creating a great

00:42:34.385 --> 00:42:36.787
business.

00:42:36.787 --> 00:42:37.788
Thank you very much.

00:42:37.788 --> 00:42:39.790
[Applause]

00:42:46.363 --> 00:42:47.531
&gt;&gt; Thank, Niels, very much.

00:42:47.531 --> 00:42:51.035
Our final topic we want to
talk about is the evolution in

00:42:51.035 --> 00:42:52.536
containers.

00:42:52.536 --> 00:42:55.606
And who better to talk
about that than Eric Brewer.

00:42:55.606 --> 00:42:57.875
Eric is a pioneer
in Cloud computing.

00:42:57.875 --> 00:42:59.343
In fact, before
coming to Google,

00:42:59.343 --> 00:43:02.246
he built one of the world's
first scalable clusters.

00:43:02.246 --> 00:43:06.183
He's been involved in industry
and academia as being on the

00:43:06.183 --> 00:43:07.651
staff at U.C. Berkeley.

00:43:07.651 --> 00:43:10.654
He's been a champion inside
Google for this notion of a move

00:43:10.654 --> 00:43:13.958
to a third wave of computing
driven by what we can do with

00:43:13.958 --> 00:43:15.092
containers.

00:43:15.092 --> 00:43:17.261
He's going to talk about
Kubernetes and he's going to

00:43:17.261 --> 00:43:20.264
give you a view of some of the
deeper implications and where we

00:43:20.264 --> 00:43:24.101
see this technology evolving
over the next few years.

00:43:24.101 --> 00:43:27.604
So would you please join me in
welcoming on stage Eric Brewer.

00:43:27.604 --> 00:43:29.273
Eric?

00:43:29.273 --> 00:43:38.415
[ Music ]

00:43:38.415 --> 00:43:38.849
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:43:38.849 --> 00:43:40.050
Thank you so much, Greg.

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:42.619
If you were to ask me
what my job was at Google,

00:43:42.619 --> 00:43:44.722
I would, having now thought
about it a little bit,

00:43:44.722 --> 00:43:47.157
say what's next?

00:43:47.157 --> 00:43:49.660
That's a fun job to have.

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:50.861
Today we'll talk
about containers,

00:43:50.861 --> 00:43:53.364
which for the past few
years have been what's next.

00:43:53.364 --> 00:43:55.165
And we've made a lot
of progress on that,

00:43:55.165 --> 00:43:58.002
especially through Kubernetes,
but I also want to talk about

00:43:58.002 --> 00:43:59.336
some things we
haven't gotten to.

00:43:59.336 --> 00:44:04.074
So I view this era as kind
of the Cloud-native era.

00:44:04.074 --> 00:44:07.378
And what I mean by that is the
applications you build are built

00:44:07.378 --> 00:44:08.946
to run natively in the Cloud.

00:44:08.946 --> 00:44:12.116
You're expecting to take use
-- make use of Cloud resources,

00:44:12.116 --> 00:44:14.852
you're expecting to have an
infinite number of machines,

00:44:14.852 --> 00:44:17.287
essentially, and things
you can easily scale.

00:44:17.287 --> 00:44:18.188
All right.

00:44:18.188 --> 00:44:19.223
So that's a wonderful
place to be.

00:44:19.223 --> 00:44:22.059
It's built on top of containers,
that's certainly an important

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:22.960
part of it.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:26.030
It's very dynamic because you
can change not only the number

00:44:26.030 --> 00:44:29.466
of services you have,
the kinds -- for example,

00:44:29.466 --> 00:44:32.503
with custom images, you can have
as much RAM or CPU as you want.

00:44:32.503 --> 00:44:34.571
There is no physical
machine like that actually.

00:44:34.571 --> 00:44:37.608
So these are literally rote
machines in that senses.

00:44:37.608 --> 00:44:41.178
And we're moving to a
model of microservices,

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:44.581
not necessarily because we
want the services per se,

00:44:44.581 --> 00:44:48.152
but we want the decoupling
that the services give you,

00:44:48.152 --> 00:44:50.721
so that you can have a lot of
different people and a lot of

00:44:50.721 --> 00:44:52.623
different teams
working well together.

00:44:52.623 --> 00:44:55.492
So this has been
enabled by Kubernetes,

00:44:55.492 --> 00:44:57.661
and I'll talk about
that some today.

00:44:57.661 --> 00:45:00.464
In particular, the way it allows
you to do production versions of

00:45:00.464 --> 00:45:02.433
containers.

00:45:02.433 --> 00:45:06.870
But one thing we haven't
really gotten to is so, fine,

00:45:06.870 --> 00:45:08.472
I can have a container,
I can schedule it,

00:45:08.472 --> 00:45:12.376
but how do I actually manage
my whole app and evolve it,

00:45:12.376 --> 00:45:14.244
and how do I configure it?

00:45:14.244 --> 00:45:16.380
Because when I think about
things that have been tricky

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:19.316
inside Google, configuration
actually is always at the top of

00:45:19.316 --> 00:45:20.150
the list.

00:45:20.150 --> 00:45:23.520
I would say when we have
software errors internally that

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:27.224
cause problems, configuration
errors are high among them,

00:45:27.224 --> 00:45:28.358
right?

00:45:28.358 --> 00:45:31.595
It's kind of a nasty little
area that's hard to get right.

00:45:31.595 --> 00:45:34.665
And I'm going to talk about
what's next for that in

00:45:34.665 --> 00:45:38.635
particular today, hopefully
make some progress.

00:45:38.635 --> 00:45:42.573
Now, we've been using containers
for more than ten years.

00:45:42.573 --> 00:45:45.342
Our definition of containers
didn't start with Docker.

00:45:45.342 --> 00:45:47.344
It predates that by a long way.

00:45:47.344 --> 00:45:49.746
It started really
with LINUX containers,

00:45:49.746 --> 00:45:51.849
which we were putting in
for performance isolation.

00:45:51.849 --> 00:45:53.450
So the first thing we want out
of containers is performance

00:45:53.450 --> 00:45:54.651
isolation.

00:45:54.651 --> 00:45:56.920
We did this through C groups,
which we contributed to LINUX

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:58.322
about 10 years ago.

00:45:58.322 --> 00:46:00.357
Name spaces have been added.

00:46:00.357 --> 00:46:03.093
There's lots of features that
are about the isolation aspects

00:46:03.093 --> 00:46:04.094
of containers.

00:46:04.094 --> 00:46:06.630
That's what gives us isolation,
so we can run many different

00:46:06.630 --> 00:46:09.566
jobs on the same physical nodes.

00:46:09.566 --> 00:46:12.903
Docker came along and did
a really nice job with the

00:46:12.903 --> 00:46:13.937
packaging.

00:46:13.937 --> 00:46:17.074
We have had packaging inside
Google for 10 years of various

00:46:17.074 --> 00:46:20.444
flavors, but I will admit the
Docker version for packaging,

00:46:20.444 --> 00:46:21.578
at least, is a little cleaner.

00:46:21.578 --> 00:46:24.548
In particular, they have an
overlay file system that allows

00:46:24.548 --> 00:46:27.885
you to compose different
pieces into a single entity,

00:46:27.885 --> 00:46:31.788
and it does a nice job with
how you handle libraries.

00:46:31.788 --> 00:46:34.424
So for example, if your
container is immutable,

00:46:34.424 --> 00:46:37.060
it includes the libraries
you need and, therefore,

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:39.796
it runs the same on a laptop
as it does on the Cloud.

00:46:39.796 --> 00:46:42.766
We'll come back to that
point about immutability,

00:46:42.766 --> 00:46:44.768
because it's something
I want to build on.

00:46:47.104 --> 00:46:52.075
On the Kubernetes front, it's
really about trying to bring --

00:46:52.075 --> 00:46:56.280
the way we think about using a
native production to the Cloud.

00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:59.449
In particular, we started
with Borg and Omega,

00:46:59.449 --> 00:47:01.718
these are -- you know, Borg is
more than 10 years old at this

00:47:01.718 --> 00:47:05.355
point, that is the scheduler
for the internal jobs at Google.

00:47:05.355 --> 00:47:07.491
But it's actually more
than just a scheduler.

00:47:07.491 --> 00:47:10.527
It's actually implicit
in its ecosystem,

00:47:10.527 --> 00:47:14.298
in things like load balancing
and naming and discovery and

00:47:14.298 --> 00:47:18.168
health checking and how
you move jobs around.

00:47:18.168 --> 00:47:21.538
So we wanted to take the lessons
from Borg and rebuild them into

00:47:21.538 --> 00:47:25.542
Kubernetes, which I think
we've done actually quite well.

00:47:25.542 --> 00:47:29.146
And then talk about how you
use that to get a Google-like

00:47:29.146 --> 00:47:30.147
experience.

00:47:30.147 --> 00:47:31.615
And I'll say what that means.

00:47:31.615 --> 00:47:35.018
In particular, the biggest
change between Borg and

00:47:35.018 --> 00:47:36.920
Kubernetes is not
the functionality,

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:40.123
it's the fact that Kubernetes
is open source, right?

00:47:40.123 --> 00:47:44.228
Borg is a complete giant
monolithic thing that we

00:47:44.228 --> 00:47:46.430
couldn't possibly open source,
and you wouldn't want it if we

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:48.198
could, actually, right?

00:47:48.198 --> 00:47:52.102
But it's a great thing to learn
from and Kubernetes takes the

00:47:52.102 --> 00:47:56.006
best of it and has really made it something that is much more

00:47:56.006 --> 00:47:58.041
usable and much cleaner.

00:47:58.041 --> 00:48:01.078
So despite being
only 20 months old,

00:48:01.078 --> 00:48:05.148
Kubernetes is already the top
.01 percent of GitHub projects,

00:48:05.148 --> 00:48:08.752
it's had 27,000 commits,
which is a crazy number.

00:48:08.752 --> 00:48:14.258
In fact, we had to add staff
just to deal with pull requests.

00:48:14.258 --> 00:48:16.093
Pull requests you get when
you have that many commits,

00:48:16.093 --> 00:48:17.194
it's crazy, right?

00:48:17.194 --> 00:48:19.997
That is a very healthy
sign of the community,

00:48:19.997 --> 00:48:22.332
and that's really the reason
we open source it is to get that

00:48:22.332 --> 00:48:26.036
community participation and
frankly to enable hybrid and

00:48:26.036 --> 00:48:27.604
multiCloud performance.

00:48:27.604 --> 00:48:33.777
So these two work together,
Docker for packaging and

00:48:33.777 --> 00:48:36.213
Kubernetes for -- for
management of containers.

00:48:36.213 --> 00:48:40.117
And so what do I think of as the
things that Kubernetes needs to

00:48:40.117 --> 00:48:42.786
provide, which are previously the things that were in the Borg

00:48:42.786 --> 00:48:44.488
ecosystem -- are in the Borg ecosystem.

00:48:44.488 --> 00:48:50.227
In particular, it's things like how you provision the nodes and

00:48:50.227 --> 00:48:51.295
attach the storage.

00:48:51.295 --> 00:48:54.598
It's how you connect to the
containers, both directly,

00:48:54.598 --> 00:48:57.234
which is actually not
the case that matters,

00:48:57.234 --> 00:48:59.236
and indirectly through
load balancing.

00:48:59.236 --> 00:49:03.373
So deep in the kind of design
of Kubernetes is how you do load

00:49:03.373 --> 00:49:04.374
balancing, right?

00:49:04.374 --> 00:49:07.644
You really kind of can't use
Kubernetes without automatically

00:49:07.644 --> 00:49:09.646
using load balancing
all over the place.

00:49:09.646 --> 00:49:12.149
And that is pretty fundamental
because load balancing is

00:49:12.149 --> 00:49:14.384
actually really
about availability.

00:49:14.384 --> 00:49:19.456
It's how we decouple which pods
are up from the name of the

00:49:19.456 --> 00:49:21.458
service that you're
using, right?

00:49:21.458 --> 00:49:23.660
So as long as the load balancer
is up, your service is up,

00:49:23.660 --> 00:49:26.396
and it can have one
pod or 10 pods or 100,

00:49:26.396 --> 00:49:29.733
and we will vary those numbers.

00:49:29.733 --> 00:49:31.234
We actively monitor the health.

00:49:31.234 --> 00:49:33.470
We find that if in
production operations,

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:36.273
you have actually know which
pods are up and running

00:49:36.273 --> 00:49:37.274
correctly.

00:49:37.274 --> 00:49:39.476
One of the changes we've made
is to make it easier for you to

00:49:39.476 --> 00:49:41.578
tell us when a pod
is working correctly.

00:49:41.578 --> 00:49:44.114
We'll ask you, is this pod
working and if the pod can say

00:49:44.114 --> 00:49:45.115
yes or no.

00:49:45.115 --> 00:49:46.116
All right.

00:49:46.116 --> 00:49:50.220
That ends up being a very
robust way to build systems.

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:52.222
Scaling, of course, is critical.

00:49:52.222 --> 00:49:54.858
This is also inherent in
the load balancer, right?

00:49:54.858 --> 00:49:56.393
So we do a lot of auto scaling.

00:49:56.393 --> 00:49:58.895
I'll talk a bit more
about that in a minute.

00:49:58.895 --> 00:50:01.164
And finally, I think,
the most important part,

00:50:01.164 --> 00:50:04.201
because what's the essential
differentiator for Kubernetes,

00:50:04.201 --> 00:50:07.671
it's really about evolution,
which is the orchestration.

00:50:07.671 --> 00:50:08.672
All right.

00:50:08.672 --> 00:50:12.376
Kubernetes makes it really easy
to have a running service and

00:50:12.376 --> 00:50:16.179
upgrade that service while
it's running to a new version.

00:50:16.179 --> 00:50:19.983
That is historically hard to do,
and a super important operation.

00:50:19.983 --> 00:50:22.552
You can't take your service down
to upgrade it, that's not okay,

00:50:22.552 --> 00:50:23.553
right?

00:50:23.553 --> 00:50:25.555
And particularly when you
get to more complex projects,

00:50:25.555 --> 00:50:29.025
you'll find you have many
different teams building parts

00:50:29.025 --> 00:50:32.696
of a larger service, and you
really want every one of those

00:50:32.696 --> 00:50:35.565
teams to have their own
independent updates.

00:50:35.565 --> 00:50:38.268
That's how you get velocity.

00:50:38.268 --> 00:50:40.270
So that's what you
want out of Kubernetes.

00:50:40.270 --> 00:50:45.976
There's a few other things I
want to get across about just

00:50:45.976 --> 00:50:48.845
the core of it that I think you
can always use a little more

00:50:48.845 --> 00:50:49.846
explanation.

00:50:49.846 --> 00:50:54.751
One of them is, this thing
that what is the cluster?

00:50:54.751 --> 00:50:58.221
And it gets to how
we think about Borg.

00:50:58.221 --> 00:51:00.991
So a pod is a collection
of containers,

00:51:00.991 --> 00:51:03.627
that's the unit of deployment.

00:51:03.627 --> 00:51:06.563
The cluster in Kubernetes is
essentially a collection of

00:51:06.563 --> 00:51:08.565
nodes that you deploy pods onto.

00:51:08.565 --> 00:51:11.568
And the Borg equivalent
of this essentially,

00:51:11.568 --> 00:51:13.570
I think of it as being
the sea of resources.

00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:15.839
When you're programming
internally at Google and you

00:51:15.839 --> 00:51:19.609
want to run your job, Borg
gives you this literal sea of

00:51:19.609 --> 00:51:21.945
thousands of places
that job can run.

00:51:21.945 --> 00:51:25.715
And you don't think at all
about specific machines.

00:51:25.715 --> 00:51:28.485
Your interface with the system
has nothing to do with machines,

00:51:28.485 --> 00:51:31.822
it has only to do with these
places that can run containers,

00:51:31.822 --> 00:51:32.889
right?

00:51:32.889 --> 00:51:34.891
That is a wonderful place to be.

00:51:34.891 --> 00:51:37.360
So the point here is if you
have this notion of a cluster,

00:51:37.360 --> 00:51:39.362
this more like a
virtual cluster,

00:51:39.362 --> 00:51:41.598
it is decoupled from the
applications that run on it.

00:51:41.598 --> 00:51:45.168
So that means that one team can
manage the environment that you

00:51:45.168 --> 00:51:49.773
run on, and another team can
actually run the application.

00:51:49.773 --> 00:51:50.774
Right?

00:51:50.774 --> 00:51:52.776
That makes all the
teams more effective.

00:51:52.776 --> 00:51:54.778
That's an important decoupling,
the environment that you run on

00:51:54.778 --> 00:51:57.347
from the actual application
you're trying to build.

00:51:57.347 --> 00:52:00.750
Of course, in Google,
like in the Cloud,

00:52:00.750 --> 00:52:03.720
we actually have one big
collection that lots of

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:05.722
different things
can run on, right?

00:52:05.722 --> 00:52:08.825
So that's actually part of
the fun of the schedule.

00:52:08.825 --> 00:52:11.461
The same decoupling
should apply for services.

00:52:11.461 --> 00:52:13.563
So when I say services
are independent,

00:52:13.563 --> 00:52:15.565
or what's the goal
of a microservice,

00:52:15.565 --> 00:52:19.436
my primary goal is that it
is independently upgradeable,

00:52:19.436 --> 00:52:22.305
that you can upgrade this
microservice even with other

00:52:22.305 --> 00:52:24.674
parts of your
application not changing,

00:52:24.674 --> 00:52:27.377
because that's what gives
different teams high velocity,

00:52:27.377 --> 00:52:30.080
so that I can have a team
that's going quite quickly here,

00:52:30.080 --> 00:52:33.216
unaffected by whatever's
going on in other teams.

00:52:33.216 --> 00:52:35.785
That's hard to do and
microservice has done well to

00:52:35.785 --> 00:52:40.590
give you that structure.

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:42.993
Oh, no, shoot.

00:52:42.993 --> 00:52:45.228
Maybe we can go back one slide.

00:52:45.228 --> 00:52:47.464
Thank you.

00:52:47.464 --> 00:52:50.700
I'm happy to
announce GCPNext 1.2.

00:52:50.700 --> 00:52:53.603
This has been a tremendous
amount of work by lots of

00:52:53.603 --> 00:52:55.372
contributors over
the past few months.

00:52:55.372 --> 00:52:57.707
It has a lot of
interesting features,

00:52:57.707 --> 00:53:00.210
I think the most
obvious one is scale.

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:02.379
You can now run jobs
that can run on 1,000

00:53:02.379 --> 00:53:05.315
nodes with up
to 30,000 pods.

00:53:05.315 --> 00:53:10.086
That is huge, well -- it's
highly requested feature,

00:53:10.086 --> 00:53:13.023
and really means that scaling is
not a practical limit for almost

00:53:13.023 --> 00:53:14.291
every team.

00:53:14.291 --> 00:53:16.893
I'd be surprised if you
need more than 30,000 pods.

00:53:16.893 --> 00:53:17.727
If you do, great.

00:53:17.727 --> 00:53:19.195
We're happy to host you.

00:53:19.195 --> 00:53:20.597
You can be our new favorites.

00:53:20.597 --> 00:53:25.068
Some of the things in 1.2, much
better control over ingress,

00:53:25.068 --> 00:53:27.938
especially layer
seven load balancing,

00:53:27.938 --> 00:53:29.906
jobs which is a way to
do batch scheduling,

00:53:29.906 --> 00:53:32.208
so now you can do batch
scheduling -- batch jobs in

00:53:32.208 --> 00:53:33.543
Kubernetes.

00:53:33.543 --> 00:53:36.446
DaemonSets are allowing you
to have a daemon running,

00:53:36.446 --> 00:53:38.615
one per node, which allows
you to do certain kind of

00:53:38.615 --> 00:53:42.252
housekeeping things
like log rotation.

00:53:42.252 --> 00:53:44.387
API to manage
running deployments,

00:53:44.387 --> 00:53:46.856
to give you aggregated
statistics, for example.

00:53:46.856 --> 00:53:48.858
And config map, which I'll
talk about in a little bit,

00:53:48.858 --> 00:53:50.860
which is a new way
to do configuration.

00:53:53.964 --> 00:53:55.799
So back to configuration.

00:53:55.799 --> 00:54:00.470
So configuration is kind of
a mess today, I would say.

00:54:00.470 --> 00:54:03.440
It's -- it's unsolved maybe.

00:54:03.440 --> 00:54:05.008
Mess is too strong a word.

00:54:05.008 --> 00:54:07.110
So we're good at
building things,

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:10.046
we have processes for that
that are pretty clean.

00:54:10.046 --> 00:54:14.050
We're pretty good now with
containers and packaging those

00:54:14.050 --> 00:54:15.652
things into runnable systems.

00:54:15.652 --> 00:54:20.624
Then when you deploy it, it's
kind of a collection of scripts

00:54:20.624 --> 00:54:24.694
and command line arguments and
things that are highly variable,

00:54:24.694 --> 00:54:27.797
and it would be nice to
see if we can improve that.

00:54:27.797 --> 00:54:32.669
So the two most
common approaches,

00:54:32.669 --> 00:54:34.371
and we use both of
these internally,

00:54:34.371 --> 00:54:37.607
are scripting and
configuration languages.

00:54:37.607 --> 00:54:40.677
Scripting is just
what -- like you say,

00:54:40.677 --> 00:54:43.847
you write scripts to actually
configure your nodes to put them

00:54:43.847 --> 00:54:45.615
in the right state and
start the software.

00:54:45.615 --> 00:54:47.250
This always works.

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:49.586
It's eminently powerful,
it can do anything,

00:54:49.586 --> 00:54:51.821
but it's very fragile, right?

00:54:51.821 --> 00:54:55.125
We find that, you know, if
something stops in the middle of

00:54:55.125 --> 00:54:58.662
an initialization script, now
you're in this weird state,

00:54:58.662 --> 00:55:00.463
you don't know how to fix it.

00:55:00.463 --> 00:55:02.465
So how do you get
back to a good state?

00:55:02.465 --> 00:55:03.733
It's very fragile.

00:55:03.733 --> 00:55:07.337
So I don't like to use scripts
running at deployment time.

00:55:07.337 --> 00:55:12.942
Another approach which is domain for the language configuration,

00:55:12.942 --> 00:55:15.545
actually we've built many of these inside Google.

00:55:15.545 --> 00:55:17.480
We don't have just
one, we have several.

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:20.984
And they are good in that
they're less verbose,

00:55:20.984 --> 00:55:23.720
they're specific
for configuration,

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:24.954
so they're very efficient.

00:55:24.954 --> 00:55:27.891
But they have problems, too.

00:55:27.891 --> 00:55:30.660
For example, if I add a new
thing I want to configure,

00:55:30.660 --> 00:55:33.263
I have to also add it to the
language before I can use the

00:55:33.263 --> 00:55:34.898
language to fix it.

00:55:34.898 --> 00:55:36.766
That's the lagging problem.

00:55:36.766 --> 00:55:38.768
The other problem is
we have a new language,

00:55:38.768 --> 00:55:41.104
you don't typically have all the
debugging support and tooling

00:55:41.104 --> 00:55:44.641
support and eMax macros and all
the other things you might want

00:55:44.641 --> 00:55:45.942
to actually make it usable.

00:55:45.942 --> 00:55:51.981
So I think we could try a new
approach that goes back to the

00:55:51.981 --> 00:55:53.983
idea of immutable
infrastructure.

00:55:53.983 --> 00:55:55.985
Because that's really what
we're getting out of containers,

00:55:55.985 --> 00:55:58.288
we're getting
immutable packaging.

00:55:58.288 --> 00:56:00.757
Even pods are a kind
of immutability.

00:56:00.757 --> 00:56:02.992
Pods give you a group of
containers that's immutable,

00:56:02.992 --> 00:56:05.261
and they also give you
immutable port numbers,

00:56:05.261 --> 00:56:07.263
which means if you
change IP addresses,

00:56:07.263 --> 00:56:09.265
all the port numbers
stay the same.

00:56:09.265 --> 00:56:10.266
It actually is quite handy.

00:56:10.266 --> 00:56:12.268
Internally at Google, we
don't get that, by the way.

00:56:12.268 --> 00:56:14.404
We run multiple jobs
on the same machine,

00:56:14.404 --> 00:56:17.173
they share IP addresses, which
means you can't count on any

00:56:17.173 --> 00:56:19.175
particular port address
being available.

00:56:19.175 --> 00:56:25.081
So -- And yesterday we saw
Spinnaker doing lots of work

00:56:25.081 --> 00:56:27.350
with this immutable
infrastructure focus for how you

00:56:27.350 --> 00:56:28.351
configure things.

00:56:28.351 --> 00:56:31.621
But really taking
it a step farther,

00:56:31.621 --> 00:56:37.193
the core new idea that's in
this talk is the structure of a

00:56:37.193 --> 00:56:40.864
multinode deployment ought
to be immutable, too.

00:56:40.864 --> 00:56:42.499
The structure, right?

00:56:42.499 --> 00:56:45.869
So in this model, you construct
a graph that is immutable,

00:56:45.869 --> 00:56:47.971
that represents what
you want to run,

00:56:47.971 --> 00:56:50.807
and then you deploy that
graph onto actual resources.

00:56:50.807 --> 00:56:54.010
So I've broken deployment into
two steps, a construction step,

00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:56.513
which is immutable, and a
deployment step which is

00:56:56.513 --> 00:56:59.349
something you do actually once
you know you have what you want.

00:57:03.319 --> 00:57:05.321
So when you think
about it this way,

00:57:05.321 --> 00:57:08.424
it's really that we'd like
to have this new model of

00:57:08.424 --> 00:57:10.426
construction, we want
a couple properties.

00:57:10.426 --> 00:57:12.929
We'd like it to be a simple
declarative framework.

00:57:12.929 --> 00:57:14.964
I don't want to use
a lot of scripting.

00:57:14.964 --> 00:57:16.966
I don't want to have a
domain-specific language,

00:57:16.966 --> 00:57:19.536
so the idea here is I'm
going to have a minimum DSL,

00:57:19.536 --> 00:57:22.806
which is really YAML plus
some -- some parameters.

00:57:22.806 --> 00:57:26.776
And if you need code, we're
going to use it to construct the

00:57:26.776 --> 00:57:30.180
graph, not at deployment
time to run on the nodes.

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:32.715
Because construction is
an offline operation.

00:57:32.715 --> 00:57:34.717
If it doesn't go
well, we can redo it.

00:57:34.717 --> 00:57:35.718
All right?

00:57:35.718 --> 00:57:37.720
We haven't actually deployed
anything when we're doing

00:57:37.720 --> 00:57:38.721
construction.

00:57:38.721 --> 00:57:40.156
So it is a low-risk operation.

00:57:40.156 --> 00:57:42.292
The second thing we need out
of construction is it has to

00:57:42.292 --> 00:57:44.994
support encapsulation and
composition so we can build

00:57:44.994 --> 00:57:46.996
complicated things that
has some modularity.

00:57:49.833 --> 00:57:52.535
Even if we did that, we would
still need some actual run time

00:57:52.535 --> 00:57:55.271
configuration because the
graph is not sufficient for

00:57:55.271 --> 00:57:56.539
deployment.

00:57:56.539 --> 00:57:58.541
In particular, two
things don't fit.

00:57:58.541 --> 00:58:00.176
One of them is keys.

00:58:00.176 --> 00:58:02.412
I don't want to put my keys
in my container, although,

00:58:02.412 --> 00:58:04.414
by the way, I see
people do that.

00:58:04.414 --> 00:58:05.415
Don't do that.

00:58:05.415 --> 00:58:06.716
Bad idea, right?

00:58:06.716 --> 00:58:09.919
People can see your images and
they can see the keys inside the

00:58:09.919 --> 00:58:10.920
image, right?

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:14.724
So the Kubernetes answer to that
is called the secret volume.

00:58:14.724 --> 00:58:18.494
And it's basically a mounted
volume in which your secrets are

00:58:18.494 --> 00:58:21.397
stored, and we connect that
volume to the right node at

00:58:21.397 --> 00:58:22.665
deployment time.

00:58:22.665 --> 00:58:25.168
So it's totally out of band
from the packaging path.

00:58:25.168 --> 00:58:28.171
That's the right model.

00:58:28.171 --> 00:58:31.941
Now, with config map in 1.2,
we're extending that idea to

00:58:31.941 --> 00:58:33.576
generalize config.

00:58:33.576 --> 00:58:35.778
So any configuration
you want for a pod,

00:58:35.778 --> 00:58:39.215
instead of passing it in through
flags or environment variables

00:58:39.215 --> 00:58:42.252
or some other path, we're
going to put it in a volume,

00:58:42.252 --> 00:58:43.653
and you mount that volume.

00:58:43.653 --> 00:58:46.923
And it has a lot of advantages.

00:58:46.923 --> 00:58:50.793
In particular, the volume itself
can now be part of construction.

00:58:50.793 --> 00:58:53.162
I can build a graph and say
these are the volumes to contain

00:58:53.162 --> 00:58:55.431
the config and the secrets
that we want to use at our

00:58:55.431 --> 00:58:56.699
deployments.

00:58:56.699 --> 00:58:59.769
We can now update config
in a reasonable way.

00:58:59.769 --> 00:59:01.905
Like if you want to update
config using environment

00:59:01.905 --> 00:59:04.641
variables or using flags, you
have to restart the service,

00:59:04.641 --> 00:59:05.942
right?

00:59:05.942 --> 00:59:08.478
In this model, it's a
volume, it's attached.

00:59:08.478 --> 00:59:11.080
You can just rescan
the volume, right,

00:59:11.080 --> 00:59:12.715
so it makes updates simpler.

00:59:12.715 --> 00:59:15.852
And finally, puts all the config
in one place that's easy to have

00:59:15.852 --> 00:59:18.955
auditing and source code
control over, right?

00:59:18.955 --> 00:59:21.791
So you can look in the
volume and see what's there,

00:59:21.791 --> 00:59:23.826
it's very clear what the
configuration of a running

00:59:23.826 --> 00:59:24.827
system is.

00:59:24.827 --> 00:59:27.497
One problem we have inside
Google is that after a job is

00:59:27.497 --> 00:59:31.567
running, we don't know how
it was configured, right?

00:59:31.567 --> 00:59:34.904
We can't introspect to see
exactly how it got set up.

00:59:34.904 --> 00:59:36.139
That's a mistake, right?

00:59:36.139 --> 00:59:38.308
We're not repeating that
mistake with Kubernetes.

00:59:41.244 --> 00:59:43.980
So now if I go back to
the flow of configuration,

00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:47.483
I would say we do our
build, we do our packaging,

00:59:47.483 --> 00:59:51.888
and now we do construction,
which is we define the topology

00:59:51.888 --> 00:59:55.858
of what we want to deploy, in
this case it has a front end and

00:59:55.858 --> 00:59:57.894
a back end with multiple nodes.

00:59:57.894 --> 01:00:01.497
By the way, I don't have to fix
the number of pods in horizontal

01:00:01.497 --> 01:00:02.498
scaling.

01:00:02.498 --> 01:00:04.500
When I say fix the structure,
I'm saying this is a

01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:07.170
variable-size front end, but I
don't have to know what the size

01:00:07.170 --> 01:00:08.171
is.

01:00:08.171 --> 01:00:09.539
That, I can change at run time.

01:00:09.539 --> 01:00:14.677
Then once I know what I want to
deploy, then I can deploy it,

01:00:14.677 --> 01:00:16.679
right, and that's
a separate step.

01:00:16.679 --> 01:00:19.282
So really we have a mounted
volumes for our secrets,

01:00:19.282 --> 01:00:25.188
we have a construction phase
and a deployment phase.

01:00:25.188 --> 01:00:27.924
This leads to the final little
bit I want to talk about,

01:00:27.924 --> 01:00:30.693
which is I just told you
conceptually about the idea of

01:00:30.693 --> 01:00:34.897
construction, but I haven't
given you any tools to do it.

01:00:34.897 --> 01:00:37.533
And so the last part is
the tools to do that.

01:00:37.533 --> 01:00:41.938
And so our approach on this
is to make these graphs be

01:00:41.938 --> 01:00:43.406
essentially a type system.

01:00:43.406 --> 01:00:45.375
So we're going to have a type
for everything you want to

01:00:45.375 --> 01:00:46.376
build.

01:00:46.376 --> 01:00:48.378
So if you want a front
end, that's a type, right?

01:00:48.378 --> 01:00:51.848
And the metabolic half
of this is constructors,

01:00:51.848 --> 01:00:53.516
as in Java or C++.

01:00:53.516 --> 01:00:56.419
So I have a constructor
with arguments.

01:00:56.419 --> 01:00:59.188
It builds an object
of that type, right?

01:00:59.188 --> 01:01:01.724
And it's a thing that runs
once at construction time,

01:01:01.724 --> 01:01:03.993
and it gives you an
immutable output,

01:01:03.993 --> 01:01:07.797
which is the
parameterized resource.

01:01:07.797 --> 01:01:10.833
So these nested types have
meanings that are useful,

01:01:10.833 --> 01:01:13.770
right, that really is, front end
is a thing with meaning.

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:16.939
You can have one team define the
front end and another team use

01:01:16.939 --> 01:01:19.075
it, because it's a
well-defined type.

01:01:21.544 --> 01:01:23.579
These graphs can get
quite complicated,

01:01:23.579 --> 01:01:26.682
and so we do use code,
but not for scripting.

01:01:26.682 --> 01:01:28.684
We'll use it at
construction time.

01:01:28.684 --> 01:01:30.686
So for anything you
want to construct,

01:01:30.686 --> 01:01:32.688
you can have
construction time code,

01:01:32.688 --> 01:01:37.727
currently Python and Jinja, that
can actually generate the graphs

01:01:37.727 --> 01:01:39.862
locally using whatever
logic they want.

01:01:39.862 --> 01:01:40.897
So that's actually hosted.

01:01:40.897 --> 01:01:43.866
You can use that to decide how
you want to build your system.

01:01:47.970 --> 01:01:51.641
The instantiation of this so far
is Google Deployment Manager.

01:01:51.641 --> 01:01:55.044
This is a new framework that
gives you essentially a way to

01:01:55.044 --> 01:01:58.614
use parameterized templates,
with this kind of recursive

01:01:58.614 --> 01:02:01.484
resource construction, which
I'll show you in a second.

01:02:01.484 --> 01:02:04.487
In particular, it supports
the construction model and the

01:02:04.487 --> 01:02:05.655
deployment model separately.

01:02:05.655 --> 01:02:09.225
It does both, but they're
actually separate things.

01:02:09.225 --> 01:02:12.895
And then what you get out of
this overall is you get the

01:02:12.895 --> 01:02:16.766
ability to have not only
front-end managers for all the

01:02:16.766 --> 01:02:18.734
Google primitives, but
you can make your own.

01:02:18.734 --> 01:02:21.104
So essentially, this is
an extendible type system.

01:02:21.104 --> 01:02:23.873
You make your own types, you
share them with your teams,

01:02:23.873 --> 01:02:26.242
and then they can
build on them, right?

01:02:26.242 --> 01:02:28.244
So here's an example.

01:02:29.579 --> 01:02:31.848
It's going back to
the constructor idea,

01:02:31.848 --> 01:02:34.183
it's really
parameterized template.

01:02:34.183 --> 01:02:38.354
So the YAML file is the
arguments to the constructor.

01:02:38.354 --> 01:02:42.058
This says, oh, this is a thing
with a front end and back end

01:02:42.058 --> 01:02:44.861
that runs in us-central1, and this is the production version,

01:02:44.861 --> 01:02:47.029
not the testing version.

01:02:47.029 --> 01:02:50.399
That is the input to
a type, a template,

01:02:50.399 --> 01:02:53.603
which when you execute,
produce this separately,

01:02:53.603 --> 01:02:55.771
a front end and a back end.

01:02:55.771 --> 01:02:57.773
And these are in black, because
I want you to think of these as

01:02:57.773 --> 01:02:58.774
black boxes.

01:02:58.774 --> 01:03:01.144
Some other team may have defined
what a front end is and a back

01:03:01.144 --> 01:03:04.514
end is, and you're
using them in your type.

01:03:04.514 --> 01:03:06.516
So we do get encapsulation.

01:03:08.784 --> 01:03:11.988
Now, expanding those out, they
are just types themselves that

01:03:11.988 --> 01:03:14.590
have parameters and templates.

01:03:14.590 --> 01:03:16.993
And they recursively
get expanded.

01:03:16.993 --> 01:03:19.262
They'll actually expand to,
oh, this is how you implement a

01:03:19.262 --> 01:03:23.166
front end, it has these kind
of nodes with these disks,

01:03:23.166 --> 01:03:25.168
has this load
balancing settings,

01:03:25.168 --> 01:03:26.269
has these firewall rules.

01:03:26.269 --> 01:03:30.006
That is inside the front end
and back end respectively.

01:03:32.208 --> 01:03:35.478
So we're getting actually
that recursive graph structure

01:03:35.478 --> 01:03:37.480
through this
construction process.

01:03:37.480 --> 01:03:40.783
Now we have an immutable
well-defined description of our

01:03:40.783 --> 01:03:44.020
deployment, and now we
just deploy it, right?

01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:47.256
And which means that will be a
repeatable process and very easy

01:03:47.256 --> 01:03:49.859
to make sure it gets
done exactly right.

01:03:49.859 --> 01:03:51.861
Very robust.

01:03:54.797 --> 01:03:57.700
I'll just go through
this relatively quickly.

01:03:57.700 --> 01:04:00.670
This does fit well with this
model of secrets and config.

01:04:00.670 --> 01:04:03.906
Because, again, the structure
includes the secrets volume and

01:04:03.906 --> 01:04:06.409
the config volume, and so
when we do the deployment,

01:04:06.409 --> 01:04:10.446
you kind of wake up and on your
pod is the data you need for

01:04:10.446 --> 01:04:13.216
configuration and
for the keys, right?

01:04:13.216 --> 01:04:15.184
They're just magically
in the right place.

01:04:17.620 --> 01:04:20.389
Finally, it's not enough that
this be something that we can

01:04:20.389 --> 01:04:23.292
use on GCP.

01:04:23.292 --> 01:04:26.362
We are the open Cloud, we
believe in open source,

01:04:26.362 --> 01:04:29.131
and this is an open
source project itself.

01:04:29.131 --> 01:04:32.902
So we've been working with
DAES on their Helm project,

01:04:32.902 --> 01:04:35.304
which we're fans of, and
we've added this construction

01:04:35.304 --> 01:04:38.140
capability working
with them to Helm,

01:04:38.140 --> 01:04:40.142
and you can go check
this out on GitHub.

01:04:40.142 --> 01:04:44.380
I would say it's almost ready,
it's definitely in progress and

01:04:44.380 --> 01:04:45.848
usable and interesting.

01:04:45.848 --> 01:04:48.918
They have a notion of a chart,
which is very similar to our

01:04:48.918 --> 01:04:52.221
type system, so we extended
that to include this kind of

01:04:52.221 --> 01:04:53.222
construction.

01:04:53.222 --> 01:04:55.291
There's an example, the helm
installed redis-cluster.

01:04:55.291 --> 01:04:58.494
That basically means,
redis-cluster is a defined type

01:04:58.494 --> 01:05:02.398
of a thing they made, and you
can install it as a thing.

01:05:02.398 --> 01:05:04.400
That is a deploy step
given the instruction.

01:05:06.669 --> 01:05:08.137
That's what's next for config.

01:05:08.137 --> 01:05:12.074
I'm super happy to be here
today, and thank you so much.

01:05:12.074 --> 01:05:18.481
[Applause]

01:05:18.481 --> 01:05:20.683
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Eric.

01:05:20.683 --> 01:05:22.251
I love -- while
Eric was talking,

01:05:22.251 --> 01:05:27.189
I saw an analyst from Redmonk
tweeted fan boy moment,

01:05:27.189 --> 01:05:29.659
Eric Brewer on stage.

01:05:29.659 --> 01:05:30.793
That's my day every
day I come to work,

01:05:30.793 --> 01:05:33.596
is I get to work with people
that are just amazing thinkers,

01:05:33.596 --> 01:05:35.298
doers and builders.

01:05:35.298 --> 01:05:39.068
That wraps up our Day
Two event this morning.

01:05:39.068 --> 01:05:42.171
I want to thank you again for
your time, for your attention,

01:05:42.171 --> 01:05:44.740
for your contributions to
everything that we do in Google

01:05:44.740 --> 01:05:45.975
Cloud Platform together.

01:05:45.975 --> 01:05:48.878
I hope you enjoy the rest
of your day here at the event.

01:05:48.878 --> 01:05:52.214
Please interact with us online
and here and throughout the

01:05:52.214 --> 01:05:53.482
coming weeks and months.

01:05:53.482 --> 01:05:55.484
Thank you and enjoy
the rest of your day.

